 store  that in this folds object  okay moving on to the next step we're  going to specify the tuning grid  we're going to do this by using the  expand.grid function  and we're going to specify we want to  search over a range of and try could be  3 to 15.  now we're ready to rock and roll from  that workflow that we created earlier  we're going to pipe to it the tune grid  function  to the resamples argument we're going to  pass the folds that we just defined  to the grid argument we're going to pass  this rf grid object we just defined  that just contains the values of mtri  we're going to search over  then we're going to specify the metrics  that we're interested in  now i'm going to specify the area under  the  precision recall curve and the area  under the  uh roc curve now we're going to pipe to  that  this collect metrics function and then  bam  we have a whole lot of different results  here  for the various values of m tri that we  were searching over  now this is a lot of super helpful  information  i see a lot of times area under the uh  precision recall curve is less than 0.5  area under the roc a curve seems to be  hovering around  0.63 or 0.64 a lot of the time  this is too much output to sift through  and we want a robust way of actually  looking through this  so i'm going to come up with a  programmatic way of finding  uh what our optimal value of m tri could  be  so i look i reported uh area under the  uh precision recall curve here before  but  maybe i'm more interested in the area  under the roc  so let's filter down to a metric of  uh roc underscore auc  now we're going to find the row which  maximizes  uh the mean uh well rock  our area under the roc in this instance  so  let's programmatically pull uh what  value of m tri that is out  then we're going to pass that to the m  tri  argument to random forest so you see  what i did there  store to this m try uh object here the  best performing and try and then  just go through uh the training process 