cal  to the steps there  so what we need here are two objects  one containing the predictions and one  containing the labels  by the way all this is coming from the  rocr package  unfortunately that's neither in the  carat framework or the tidy models  framework but  it's the best visualization package for  roc curves at this time that i'm  personally aware of  so for the predictions object the syntax  here is a little bit  strange because it's not super clear  well do we want the probabilities for  being  in the good class or the bad class so  it's not super clear  but so you'll have to trial and error it  a little bit  but typically i've found if you pass  the probability predictions for the  majority class to it  that that's the one that it wants so  again just trial and error this if you  run into problems  then obviously for the labels that's the  source of truth  that's the class variable from the test  set in our case here  so these two objects are going to get  passed to the prediction function  again this comes from the rocr package  so we're going to create this object  here called uh pred  okay well that's going to get passed to  this next function performance which uh  that creates an object based on the  metrics that we want  to actually visualize so  think about what an roc curve actually  is  an roc curve is going to visualize the  true positive rate  that is sensitivity against the false  positive rate  that is one minus specificity  so if you go into the help documentation  for the performance function you can  find  all the different metrics that you can  visualize but i'm going to pass  as arguments to the performance function  tpr  for true positive rate and fpr for false  positive rate  so we're going to create a plot of that  we're going to just specify to this  argument average equals  threshold and then colorize equals true  now you're probably going to know that  your roc curve  looks appropriate if uh the body of the  curve  is over the dia