eters cost and  rbf sigma which again we're going to to  both of those later  the engine is going to be the current  lab  library which again we loaded at the  beginning now for each of these models  we need to create  a workflow the random force workflow has  already been defined  so the workflow for k-nearest neighbors  and support vector machines  it's totally uh straightforward  we're going to specify the down recipe  which we created before for the random  forest  the same thing is going to work for k  nearest neighbors and support vector  machines  just pipe after the recipe the models  that we defined and  we are ready to go so all this code is  going to look remarkably similar to what  we just ran before  the only difference is for later we are  going to need this control stack  grid thing here so that's just another  argument that's going to go to the tune  grid function  but now here instead of manually  specifying the range of values that we  want to search for over the hyper  parameters  we're just going to specify just pick uh  just be smart about it  and just specify the number of different  values of hyperparameters we want to  search over  so if you know uh the tune length  of uh argument from carrot  it's just analogous to that we're just  specifying just  try seven different values for hyper  parameters for each of these  uh classifiers so i'm also not even  going to look over  uh the area under the precision recall  curve  all i want to do is find the uh  value of this of these hyper parameters  that maximize  area under the receiver operating  characteristic curve  okay go from there the syntax looks just  like it did before  all of this so far has largely just been  a set up to the real meat and potatoes  of the stax package  and that's what we're going to get into  here so  stacks much like themis as we saw  earlier for the down sampling and the up  sampling  this is another package outside of the  core tidy models ecosystem  that you are going to have to load  separately from tidy models  but let's get into the real beauty from  this package  so to start stacking the predictions  from various classifiers  we have to start by defining a data  stack object  so the this this type of code is  analogous to like  initializing a ggplot object using the  ggplot function  we're going to start by initializing  this with the stacks function  and then pipe to it uh  various candidates so the candidates are  all these definitions for  classifiers that we defined up above  so all these re-samples that we defined  for the random forest the k  ears neighbors and the support vector  machines where you have  various different configurations for  hyper parameters  all of those are candidates and they're  going to get piped to this data stack  object up top here  makes sense and you can just call the  credit stack object which  i just defined up here and it'll just  give you some basic information  it's just a data stack with three model  definitions  and 21 candidate members seven model  configurations for random forest for k  nearest neighbors for support vector  machines etc etc  the next step is we're actually going to  blend the predictions from these various  candidates  we're going to do this using an aptly  named blend predictions function  now how this works is it evaluates your  data stack by fitting a regularized  model on the assessment predictions from  each candidate member  and to do this it uses a lasso model  so lasso being a common regularization  technique  it's going to assign each of these  candidates  a beta parameter or a weight and now  if the weight is greater than zero then  that candidate gets kept  if the weight is equal to zero then that  candidate gets dropped  so the argument that you do need to  specify here is the penalty factor  and if we look at the documentation here  it can range all the way from uh  10 to the negative six all the way all  the way up to  uh 10 to the negative one so i'm 